{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed2094082456/7/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"fc4aaf52\" lines=\"17-34\">\n<description>\nSplit the two-color object horizontally, swap its colors, and slide the upper half to the right.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one background color and exactly two foreground colors.\n- All foreground pixels form one 4-connected object with an even-height bbox.\n- The object has foreground pixels on both rows adjacent to its horizontal midpoint.\n\nRule:\n- Split the object into upper and lower halves at the midpoint of its bbox.\n- Swap the two foreground colors in both halves.\n- Keep the lower half in place and shift the upper half right until its lower-left\n  boundary pixel is diagonally adjacent to the lower half's upper-right boundary pixel.\n\nKey insight:\n- The object is not copied or reflected; it is broken at the horizontal waist,\n  recolored by exchanging the two visible object colors, then rejoined diagonally.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"142ca369\" lines=\"6-19\">\n<description>\nTrace diagonal rays from triangular arrows, reflecting off colored wall pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg grid with monochrome 3-pixel L-shaped \"arrows\" (2x2 bbox missing one corner) and scattered colored wall pixels.\n\nRule:\n- Each arrow fires a ray diagonally away from its missing corner, painting bg cells in its color.\n- A head-on wall hit reverses both axes; a side-touch wall hit flips the touched axis only.\n- On any bounce the ray adopts the wall's color and recolors the contact cell.\n- Ray stops when it exits the grid or reaches another arrow.\n\nKey insight: Two distinct reflection types (corner vs side-touch) plus color inheritance at each bounce drive the path geometry.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"2e65ae53\" lines=\"88-103\">\n<description>\nFill every lattice slot from the color assigned to that slot position by the other lattices.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- The largest foreground color forms several one-pixel-thick rectangular lattice frames.\n- The frame lines divide each lattice into the same row-by-column set of rectangular slots; some slots already contain one non-frame color.\n\nRule:\n- Infer a color for each logical slot position from the colored slots across all lattice frames.\n- Repaint every slot interior in every lattice with the inferred color for its row/column position.\n- Keep the frame lines and all background outside frames unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- Slot sizes vary by frame, but the complete color pattern is indexed by lattice slot position rather than by absolute pixel size or location.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"66ac4c3b\" lines=\"57-72\">\n<description>\nComplete the sparse-color reflected copy of the foreground motif across its matching marker row or column.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has one background color and two foreground colors.\n- One foreground color appears only as a sparse marker row or column.\n- The other foreground color contains the same sparse marker positions on a parallel row or column, plus the motif on the far side of the sparse markers.\n\nRule\n- Keep the original grid unchanged.\n- Reflect every motif pixel of the larger foreground color across the two parallel marker lines.\n- Paint the reflected motif pixels with the sparse marker color.\n\nKey insight\n- The two equal marker rows or columns are paired landmarks: the sparse-color marker line borders the source motif, and the matching source-color marker line anchors the reflected copy.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"8618d23e\" lines=\"9-25\">\n<description>\nStagger the two horizontal input regions onto a larger maroon separator canvas.\n\nInput structure:\n- The input is a rectangular grid with an even number of rows.\n- The top half and bottom half are the two regions to preserve.\n- Maroon is absent from the input and is used only as the new separator color.\n\nRule:\n- Create an output with one extra row and one extra column, filled with maroon.\n- Copy the top half into the upper-left corner.\n- Copy the bottom half one row below the top region and one column to the right.\n\nKey insight:\nThe output is not a recoloring of either region; it inserts an L-shaped maroon\ngap by diagonally offsetting the lower half from the upper half.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"42f83767\" lines=\"23-39\">\n<description>\nExpand the lower color grid by replacing each pixel with its matching top stencil.\n\nInput structure\nThe input has a top legend and a lower dense color grid, separated by blank rows.\nThe legend contains left-to-right 2x2 color swatches and, to their right,\nthe same number of square neutral-color stencil masks.\n\nRule\nPair the swatches and stencil masks by left-to-right order. For every pixel in\nthe lower color grid, paste the paired stencil mask as one output block,\nrecoloring the mask pixels with that pixel's color and leaving mask holes as bg.\n\nKey insight\nThe lower grid is not copied directly; it is an index grid whose colors select\nthe stencil shape from the legend, so each input pixel becomes one square block.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"dd6b8c4b\" lines=\"9-22\">\n<description>\nMove the closest reachable maroon pixels into the 3×3 block around the unique red center.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = orange floor, magenta walls, scattered maroon (9) pixels.\n- A unique red (2) pixel marks the center of a 3×3 destination block.\n\nRule:\n- Maroon pixels connected to the destination through floor/maroon cells (not blocked by walls) are candidates.\n- Rank candidates by distance to the destination; the closest ones fill the block in row-major order.\n- Moved maroons are erased (replaced with floor color).\n\nKey insight: Wall barriers restrict connectivity — only maroon pixels reachable via flood-fill through the floor qualify, so proximity alone isn't enough.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7b0280bc\" lines=\"54-69\">\n<description>\nRecolor the shortest path between two terminal squares in a square-and-track graph.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Two non-bg colors form solid NxN squares (N>=2): one color marks exactly 2 terminals, the other marks stations.\n- A third non-bg color forms 1-cell-wide track segments connecting pairs of squares (graph edges).\n\nRule:\n- Find the shortest path (fewest edges) between the two terminal squares.\n- Recolor every station square on that path to green.\n- Recolor every track segment along that path to grey.\n- Everything else unchanged.\n\nKey insight: The grid encodes a graph where squares are nodes and tracks are edges; the task is graph shortest-path, not spatial proximity.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"d6542281\" lines=\"72-91\">\n<description>\nComplete each partial marker mask by stamping the matching multicolor sprite over it.\n\nInput structure\n- A dominant background contains one or more 8-connected multicolor sprites.\n- Separate target masks use colors that already occur inside one of those sprites.\n- A target mask may contain only one marker pixel or several same-alignment marker pixels.\n\nRule\n- For each multicolor sprite, test every translation that aligns a target pixel with a\n  same-color sprite pixel.\n- Keep the translations with maximal exact overlap against the existing target pixels,\n  rejecting translations that conflict with other foreground or overlap a source sprite.\n- Stamp the whole sprite at those translations; if a non-marker sprite color occurs as\n  a single source pixel, consume that source-only marker back to background.\n\nKey insight\nThe scattered target pixels are partial copies: their same-color overlap with a sprite\ndetermines where the full sprite must be completed.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"22425bda\" lines=\"58-76\">\n<description>\nList the colors of overlaid straight lines in their only possible bottom-to-top crossing order.\n\nInput structure\nThe background is the dominant color. Every foreground color is the visible part\nof one full one-cell-thick horizontal, vertical, or 45-degree diagonal line.\nHigher lines may hide pixels of lower lines at crossings.\n\nRule\nRecover each full line from its visible collinear pixels. At any cell where\nmultiple recovered lines cross, the visible color is above every other line\nthrough that cell. Return the unique ordering of all line colors that respects\nthose above/below constraints and whose consecutive colors are lines that\ngeometrically cross.\n\nKey insight\nThe visible crossing colors give a partial layer order, and the intended answer\nis the unique Hamiltonian chain through the line-intersection graph from the\nbottom layer to the top layer.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"97c75046\" lines=\"88-107\">\n<description>\nMove the single marker to the endpoint reached by sliding along the obstacle contour.\n\nInput structure:\n- A dominant background color fills the canvas.\n- One single-pixel marker sits away from a connected obstacle region of the\n  remaining color.\n- The marker has exactly one horizontal or vertical sightline to the obstacle.\n\nRule:\n- Find the last background pixel before the obstacle along that sightline.\n- From that pixel, try the two diagonal turns that continue around the\n  obstacle boundary.\n- Move the marker to the endpoint of the turn that remains 4-adjacent to the\n  obstacle for the longest distance.\n\nKey insight:\nThe marker is not placed by distance to the obstacle; it first points to the\nobstacle, then follows the visible outside contour until that contour segment ends.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"6d1d5c90\" lines=\"8-23\">\n<description>\nRemove the left control column and roll the remaining image downward by the red marker's row.\n\nInput structure\n- The input is a rectangular grid with a one-cell-wide control column on the left.\n- That control column is all pink except for exactly one red marker pixel.\n- The columns to the right are the image that remains in the output.\n\nRule\n- Delete the left control column.\n- Let r be the row index of the red marker in that column.\n- Cyclically shift the remaining image downward by r rows.\n\nKey insight\n- The red marker's vertical position encodes the row-roll amount; the pink\n  column is only a control strip and is removed after reading it.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9f669b64\" lines=\"26-48\">\n<description>\nPush the central solid rectangle to the grid edge, splitting the perpendicular target rectangle around its path.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- There are exactly three monochrome foreground objects arranged on one\n  horizontal or vertical axis.\n- The middle object on that axis is a solid rectangular moving object.\n- One end object is a solid rectangle or bar whose long side is perpendicular\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
